# MODULE 6 — FINANCIAL RATIO ANALYSIS

Create a section named:

FINANCIAL RATIO ANALYSIS

Divide the ratios into four categories.

### 6.1 LIQUIDITY RATIOS

Current Ratio:

Current Assets / Current Liabilities

Quick Ratio:

(Current Assets − Inventory − Other Non-Quick Current Assets) / Current Liabilities

Cash Ratio:

Cash and Cash Equivalents / Current Liabilities

Working Capital:

Current Assets − Current Liabilities

### 6.2 PROFITABILITY RATIOS

Gross Profit Margin:

Gross Profit / Net Revenue × 100

Net Profit Margin:

Net Profit / Net Revenue × 100

Operating Profit Margin:

Operating Profit / Net Revenue × 100

Return on Equity:

Profit After Tax / Average Equity × 100

Return on Capital Employed:

EBIT / Average Capital Employed × 100

### 6.3 EFFICIENCY RATIOS

Inventory Turnover:

Cost of Goods Sold / Average Inventory

Receivables Turnover:

Net Credit Sales / Average Trade Receivables

Payables Turnover:

Credit Purchases / Average Trade Payables

Debtor Collection Period:

Average Trade Receivables / Net Credit Sales × Days in Period

Creditor Payment Period:

Average Trade Payables / Credit Purchases × Days in Period

### 6.4 SOLVENCY RATIOS

Debt Equity Ratio:

Interest-Bearing Borrowings / Equity

Interest Coverage Ratio:

EBIT / Finance Costs attributable to interest

Debt Service Coverage Ratio:

Calculate only when debt-service schedules and a defined cash-available-for-debt-service measure are supplied.

Do not derive DSCR from balance sheet totals alone.

### 6.5 REQUIREMENTS

- Provide a documented numerator and denominator.
- Show the exact underlying account mapping.
- Use period-average balances where appropriate.
- Handle zero and negative denominators explicitly.
- Allow accounting policy and ratio-definition configuration.
- Show current and prior-period values.
- Highlight material changes.
- Allow monthly and annual ratio charts.
- Explain unusual movements with supporting data.

Do not assume every business or industry uses identical definitions.

